<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Hohenzollern</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hohenzollerns, Hohenzollern dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Hohenzollern is a German dynasty named after its castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. Its Franconian line, descended from Frederick of Zollern, burgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> from 1192, gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1415 and later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and, from 1871 to 1918, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. A Swabian line kept the principalities of Hohenzollern, and from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> branch came the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern.jpg" alt="House of Hohenzollern" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Намѣсникъ · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wappen_Hohenzollern_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family came from the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> in the late 11th century and took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenzollern-castle">Hohenzollern Castle</a>, on an 855-metre mountain in the Swabian Alps. Its first ancestor mentioned is Burkhard I, Count of Zollern, who according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berthold-of-reichenau">Berthold of Reichenau</a> was born before 1025 and died in 1061. A founder named Tassilo, said to be a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, was invented in 1560 by the historian Johann Basilius Herold, and his existence was disproved in the 19th century.</p><p>The Zollerns received the title of count from Emperor Henry V in 1111, and the county was called Hohenzollern from 1218. The family split into a Catholic Swabian branch and a Protestant Franconian branch, the latter founded by Conrad I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgrave of Nuremberg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Franconian Hohenzollerns supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers and were rewarded with territory, adding Ansbach in 1331 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> in 1340. In 1411 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick VI, Burgrave of Nuremberg</a>, was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> in 1415 King Sigismund made him Elector and Margrave as Frederick I; in 1417 he bought Brandenburg for 400,000 Hungarian guilders. In 1525 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Prussia</a>, last Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, became the first Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> were ruled together from 1618. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg">John Sigismund</a> turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, taking communion in the Calvinist rite on 25 December 1613, but strong protest forced him in 1615 to give up forcing his subjects to follow, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> a bi-confessional state. In 1701 Frederick I took the title King in Prussia with the assent of Emperor Leopold I. In 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was proclaimed, and William I, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> and King of Prussia, ruled it until the revolution that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in 1918 ended the German and Prussian monarchy.</p><p>The Swabian branch, founded by Frederick IV, Count of Zollern, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haigerloch">Haigerloch</a> and became princes in 1623. Troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenzollern-castle">Hohenzollern Castle</a> in 1423. This branch ruled until 1849. In 1866 Prince Charles of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen became prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and king as Carol I in 1881; his line reigned until 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From a minor princely family the Franconian line became one of the most important dynasties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by marriage and by purchasing neighbouring lands. In 1870 the candidacy of Leopold, Prince of Hohenzollern, for the Spanish throne, and Bismarck's altered Ems telegram, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. Since 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s Basic Law recognises no Hohenzollern claim to royal or imperial prerogatives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenzollern-castle">Hohenzollern Castle</a> still belongs to the family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Hohenzollern” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts sat with the Swabian counts in the Imperial Diet, belonged to the Swabian Imperial Circle and increasingly used the name <b>Hohenzollern</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Their Grand Master, Albert, of a cadet branch of the <b>House of Hohenzollern</b>, had lost a war against the Kingdom of Poland and lacked the military means to assert the order's authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first <b>Hohenzollern</b>, Frederick I, made his authority real by breaking the castles of robber barons such as the Quitzow family in 1414 with heavy artillery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first family, the Raabs, died out around 1190, and Frederick of Zollern, a Swabian noble married to the Raabs' heiress, became burgrave in 1192, founding the <b>Franconian Hohenzollern</b> line.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgraviate of Nuremberg</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Jülich succession crisis, the Treaty of Xanten in 1614 gave Cleves to Brandenburg, giving the <b>Hohenzollerns</b> their first foothold in the west.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a></li><li><blockquote>His <b>Hohenzollern</b> heirs held it until 1621, when Johann Georg lost it for supporting the Bohemian revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krnov">Duchy of Krnov</a></li></ul></section>
